---
import ExampleSource from '../../components/ExampleSource.astro';
import Sample from '../../examples/tabletop/app';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { samplesSource } from '../../lib/links';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/site-path';
import wireSource from '@opsydyn/effect-three-samples/src/tabletop/wire.ts?raw';
import modelSource from '@opsydyn/effect-three-samples/src/tabletop/model.ts?raw';
import updateSource from '@opsydyn/effect-three-samples/src/tabletop/update.ts?raw';
import sceneSource from '@opsydyn/effect-three-samples/src/tabletop/scene.ts?raw';
import viewSource from '@opsydyn/effect-three-samples/src/tabletop/view.ts?raw';
import environmentSource from '@opsydyn/effect-three-samples/src/tabletop/environment.ts?raw';
const sources = [{ name: 'wire.ts', content: wireSource }, { name: 'model.ts', content: modelSource }, { name: 'update.ts', content: updateSource }, { name: 'scene.ts', content: sceneSource }, { name: 'view.ts', content: viewSource }, { name: 'environment.ts', content: environmentSource }];
---
<Layout title="Tabletop — effect-three">
  <section class="page-intro"><a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/')}>← All examples</a><p class="eyebrow">PLAYING TOGETHER</p><h1>One world, every peer.</h1><p>Click the table or the tower to throw a ball at it, whenever you like. Open this page in a second window and throw from both: each throw goes through a lockstep session and lands at the same physics step in every window, so the tower falls the same way everywhere. Every second each window shares a checksum of its world, and the readout counts the checks that agreed. To play from another machine, run <code>bun run relay</code> from the repository and open this page with <code>?relay=ws://host:4325/any-room</code> on each.</p></section>
  <Sample client:load noMeta />
  <noscript><p>This example needs JavaScript and WebGL to render the scene.</p></noscript>
  <section class="closing-callout"><h2>The Model keeps the clock.</h2><p>The world steps only as far as <code>physics(&#123; until &#125;)</code> lets it, and says when it gets there. Throws are inputs stamped with the step they land at, kept in a <code>Timeline</code>: the Model hands them to the world while it waits at that step, so how the frames fell on each machine changes nothing. A newcomer gets the whole record and replays it, with Rapier's deterministic build.</p><a class="text-link" href={samplesSource + '/tabletop'}>Explore the example source →</a></section>
  <ExampleSource files={sources} />
</Layout>
